Chronic Pancreatitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic epigastric pain, Steatorrhoea, Brittle diabetes mellitus, Pancreatic calcifications on imaging

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-010
Reason for Visit: Severe, unrelenting flare of chronic abdominal pain radiating to the back, uncontrolled by usual oral analgesia, accompanied by significant weight loss.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a severe exacerbation of chronic epigastric pain in a patient with known chronic pancreatitis, completely refractory to standard outpatient analgesia. Urgent out-of-hours evaluation was required to exclude an acute complication (e.g., pseudocyst formation, acute-on-chronic pancreatitis), administer potent parenteral analgesia, and determine if inpatient admission for pain control and nutritional support was necessary prior to routine clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My usual painkillers aren't touching this; the pain is gnawing a hole straight through my stomach to my back, and I'm losing weight every day."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing history of chronic, continuous dull epigastric pain radiating to the back, with a severe exacerbation over the past 48 hours following a large, fatty meal. Patient notes profound, unintentional weight loss (8kg over 3 months) despite a preserved appetite. Reports bulky, foul-smelling, floating stools (steatorrhoea). Known history of brittle diabetes mellitus and chronic heavy alcohol abuse (currently abstinent for 2 years).
Functional Status: Ambulating, but frequently bending forward at the waist to seek pain relief.
Social/Safety Concerns: Risk of opioid escalation/misuse due to severe, chronic pain. Poor nutritional status.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 | HR: 100 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 55 Kg (Cachectic)
General: Patient appears chronically ill, malnourished, and in significant distress, adopting a leaning-forward posture.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals significant epigastric tenderness on deep palpation without focal peritonism, guarding, or rebound tenderness. No palpable abdominal mass. Bowel sounds are active. No clinical jaundice. Clinical features align with the tetrad: chronic epigastric pain, steatorrhoea, and brittle diabetes mellitus; pancreatic calcifications are presumed present on previous imaging.
Environmental Observations: Multiple boxes of prescribed opioid analgesics and pancreatic enzyme supplements visible.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with Creon (pancrelipase) and basal-bolus insulin. Currently maximizing prescribed oral oxycodone without effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Pancreatitis - Status: Exacerbated (Severe pain crisis)
Differentials:

Pancreatic Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of new-onset painless jaundice or a palpable mass, though the significant weight loss warrants urgent outpatient imaging to definitively exclude this complication.

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a rigid, board-like abdomen and the chronic, steady nature of the pain rather than sudden, catastrophic collapse.

Acute Pancreatitis (De Novo) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's established history of chronic structural pancreatic disease and exocrine failure, representing an acute-on-chronic flare rather than a first-time acute inflammatory event.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Complex chronic disease management)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, refractory pain crisis superimposed on advanced chronic pancreatitis, complicated by significant malabsorption and cachexia. The failure of high-dose outpatient opioid analgesia necessitated an urgent after-hours home assessment to provide immediate parenteral pain relief and formally assess for acute surgical complications, such as an expanding pancreatic pseudocyst or biliary obstruction. Delaying assessment would have resulted in intolerable suffering and potential accidental opioid overdose through desperate self-medication. The clinical presentation demands immediate escalation of analgesia and expedited imaging.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Buprenorphine 0.3mg and IM Ketorolac 30mg. Advised immediate presentation to the Emergency Department for multidisciplinary pain management, IV hydration, and an urgent CT abdomen to rule out complications.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take your pancreatic enzyme capsules exactly in the middle of your meal, rather than before or after. This ensures the artificial enzymes mix thoroughly with the food bolus in your stomach, maximizing nutrient absorption and directly reducing the bulky, foul-smelling diarrhoea you are experiencing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to chronic pain clinic and community dietician pending hospital discharge.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review 3 days post-hospital discharge to coordinate multidisciplinary care.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate hospital transfer recommended for pain crisis management. If patient declines, they must present to ED immediately if they develop high fevers, intractable vomiting, or sudden, tearing abdominal pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Severe pain flares in chronic pancreatitis often require hospitalization to break the pain cycle and rule out new complications.
Clinical Course: The gland is irreversibly damaged and scarred. Management focuses on controlling symptoms, replacing enzymes, and managing diabetes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Your current pain levels cannot be managed safely at home. If you start vomiting uncontrollably, develop a fever, or if the pain changes to a sudden, sharp, tearing sensation, you must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Years of inflammation have left your pancreas scarred and calcified. It no longer produces enough enzymes to digest fat or enough insulin to control your blood sugar. The acute pain flare is due to increased pressure in the scarred pancreatic ducts, requiring stronger hospital-level medications to settle.
Actionable Steps: Completely avoid any alcohol and adhere strictly to a very low-fat diet. Eating high-fat meals forces your damaged pancreas to attempt to produce enzymes it cannot make, directly increasing the pressure and severe pain in your abdomen.

